From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 15:49:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] flow: FLOW_INPUT_VARIANT, a stage reads the previous
 stage's result from another variant
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Before, a variant that wanted another variant's synthesis or placement
had to copy that variant's results into its own directory first.

Now FLOW_INPUT_VARIANT names the variant a stage reads the previous
stage's result from: INPUT_RESULTS_DIR. The six places a stage reads
its input say so explicitly, in its script (load_stage_input) and in
its Makefile prerequisite; every other read stays in RESULTS_DIR. It
defaults to FLOW_VARIANT, which leaves every path unchanged.

When it names another variant, load_stage_input stops if the input is
in both variants: one invocation that ran two stages, or a copy left
from an earlier run, where reading the other variant's copy would mix
results. It also stops with AUTO_MEMORIES=1: the memories are read from
the variant that synthesised them.

Carried in bazel-orfs for ORFS #4812, on 0212 (#4810) and on 0199
(load_design -backing_file, which load_design_from takes here). One
change beyond #4812 as posted: density_fill.tcl's USE_FILL=0 branch
copies 5_route from INPUT_RESULTS_DIR, as global_place_skip_io.tcl
does for GPL_PLACE_IOS; #4812 copies it from RESULTS_DIR, and a fork at
final finds nothing there. Not upstreamed here; retires with the
//:bump onto an ORFS that has #4812 with that fix.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
